\section*{Petty’s Projection-Body Conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 088; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that ellipsoids uniquely minimize normalized projection-body volume among convex bodies in every dimension at least four.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
Let $\Pi K$ be the projection body, whose support function at unit $u$ equals the $(n-1)$-volume of the orthogonal projection of $K$, and let $\kappa_j$ be the volume of the Euclidean unit ball in $\mathbb R^j$. For every $n\ge4$ and every convex body $K\subset\mathbb R^n$, the manuscript claims
\[
 \frac{|\Pi K|}{|K|^{n-1}}\ge\kappa_{n-1}^{\,n}\kappa_n^{\,2-n}.
\]
Equality is claimed exactly for $K=a+TB_2^n$ with $T$ invertible. The dimension range starts at four; the theorem does not itself supply missing lower-dimensional statements.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 088; 2026-09-24), directional projection data are encoded in a measure associated with the body's gauge. A spherical harmonic sign estimate and a variational affine-position argument give a strict norm inequality, whose equality conditions identify ellipsoids.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The linked Petty theorem begins in dimension four, whereas this statement also includes dimension three. Its ellipsoid minimum therefore covers the higher-dimensional clause without supplying the remaining case from this manuscript.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 088; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: ellipsoids uniquely minimize normalized projection-body volume among convex bodies in every dimension at least four.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{PettyProjectionVolume.lean}, \texttt{ProjectionCounterexample.lean}, \texttt{ProjectionVolume.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.Paper092.product\_counterexample}, \texttt{OAI.PettyProjection.petty\_projection\_volume}, \texttt{OAI.ProjectionCounterexample.universal\_simplex\_upper\_bound\_false}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

